# Kernel space (amix-dlm C2-2/C2-3): catalogued in the #ext kpkg block, never
# as an ordinary entry; installs /stand/k46/{unix,kabi} and the cut's ufs
# module at /etc/conf/mod.d (C2-3 v8 sec 1).  The module kernel (family
# generic, kabi k46.1.4), build id 68060-261101-57 (uname -m, kinfo); its own
# package, not a revision of a static family (C2-3 sec 1.1).
APKG_DESC=The 68040/68060 module kernel (kabi k46.1.4, loader 2): /stand/k46/unix (ET_REL) + kabi, with no built-in ufs or s5: it carries the ufs module built with it (/etc/conf/mod.d); drivers such as z3660eth come as module packages; kbootset composes them into a boot generation.  Upgrade together with kbootset -34 or later, and upgrade the module packages with it: every k46 module built for an earlier kernel is refused by kbootset (gate 2) against this one.  s5 filesystems no longer mount on the 68040/68060 line: s5 is not safe with its 4 KiB pages; kernel -82 had s5 built in.  A ufs with blocks under 4 KiB is refused at mount on the 68040/68060 line.  New in kabi 1.3: /dev/paula support (the paula-k46 module package), and a /dev/audio open that fails at once (EIO) when Paula never interrupts instead of hanging the box.  New in -52: shrinking a big file (ftruncate, tar --delete, dd seek=) no longer panics the kernel; a file shrunk and then regrown sparsely no longer reads its old bytes back; a STREAMS queue still scheduled is no longer freed under the scheduler, so a unix-socket server under client traffic (prngd with prngd-ctl) no longer panics the kernel.  New in -53: the kernel knows the random module's major (c 48), so the random module package (/dev/random, /dev/urandom) loads.  New in -55 (kabi 1.4): a filesystem module can be a demand module that loads at its first mount, and loading a module on first use no longer waits for free memory; the copy of its file is freed before the load.  New in -56: a user process can no longer read or write physical memory below 1 GiB (chip RAM, the custom chips, the kernel); a NULL pointer behaves as on the 68030; a mapping at 0-256 MiB no longer hangs or panics the kernel; swap -a of an area too big for the kernel's map fails at once (Not enough space) instead of hanging; the default data and address-space limits are unlimited; device majors come from the boot set (kbootset -34).  New in -57: an idle machine's time is counted as idle (sysinfo, sar, the process times), so fsflush no longer appears to use half the CPU.
DESC=The module kernel of the 040/060 line (the k46 kernel with ufs and s5 carved out): KD060-Z with its static Z3660 ethernet, RTG, monitor-switch and SCSI drivers taken out and the amix-dlm module core (core b736c59 + 51 patches, amix ac168e5: no-sleep root pass, the halt hook, block rows, sd_register, the arena load path, dlm_marc_root, match= at marc_root, root_defer, the static-controller skip, ramdisk_image, vfs_register and the grown vfssw, DDI 1.2's DMA names, the filesystem layouts in the abi= fingerprint, the 13 names and the 100 core-tier names a filesystem module imports, the rootfs promotion, the safe boot modules; the arena 16-aligned; the build id taken without the stamp's leading blank, so a core-tier module built with this cut loads; DDI minor 3, kabi k46.1.3: AllocMem, FreeMem, displaytype, paula_busy and audio_nopen for the paula module, the kernel listing only the names of its own minor; the major registry with c 48 random; kabi k46.1.4: a demand-class filesystem module loads at its first mount; a first-use load never waits for memory; the module majors are the booted manifest's major records) linked in with the four-card sd layer and bdevsw row 20 freed, the stock audio driver patched by amix-paula 62fdcab (MIT: while /dev/paula is open /dev/audio refuses its open and the other way round; a safe close after a killed player; the open's wait for Paula's first interrupt is bounded, so a silent Paula fails the open with EIO instead of spinning), built in amix-040-060-port (k46-module 5d6f586, cut 68060-261101-57 from public inputs only, assembled with gcc14's binutils, compiled with gcc-cross-amix 33ebadc; ITT0/DTT0 supervisor-only; the idle override's own waitloc, so an idle tick is counted as idle; the kzero fault catch, the STREAMS freeq guard: qdetach, strclose and stropen free a queue through freeq_guarded; the image stamped with its own 68060 build id; rth1 takes the loader record's root selector, a RAM-disk root, swap /dev/fakeswap, rootfstype, and a motherboard controller's record by its kinfo id, rth1=2), shipped as the ET_REL the amix boot loader binds; the Z3660 root comes from the z3660scsi root module package (root= mobo:a3000-wd,zorro:0202:01,zorro:0202:03).  Build -57 (-56 with idle ticks counted as idle; -56 was -55 with core patches 0050 and 0051 and user access below 1 GiB closed; -55 was -53 with core patches 0048 and 0049, kabi k46.1.4; -53 was -52 with c 48 random and gcc14's binutils; -52 was -61 with the kzero fault catch, the ufs tail zeroing and the freeq guard) has no built-in ufs or s5 filesystem: the package carries the ufs module as a core-tier module built with this cut (/etc/conf/mod.d/ufs, a comment-only /etc/conf/modreg.d/ufs), and the sidecar names no rootfs=; kbootset promotes ufs to a root record.  The ufs module zeroes the tail of a shrunk file's last page (ufs_vptrunz) and refuses at mount (EINVAL) a ufs whose block size is under 4 KiB, the page size of this line; -50 mounted a 2 KiB-block ufs and panicked in ufs_getapage on it.  s5 is not available on this line: s5 filesystems no longer mount on it (unsafe under its 4 KiB pages; kernel -82 had s5 built in).  /stand/k46/kabi is the kernel build's sidecar (kabi=k46.1.4, family=generic, loader-min=2, arena, ident, ksym, abi=, sdcards=, root=, rth1=2), copied verbatim.  Upgrade it together with kbootset -34 or later (it writes the major records this kernel takes its module majors from; since -30 the registry knows c 48 random, amix a8719fb; since -22, amix-dlm 1308c0b: a motherboard root record by its kinfo id for an rth1=2 kernel, and the core-tier module's tier= and build= in the root record).  The package writes no boot store itself; after the install apkg runs kbootset build and commit (or prints the command when bootset=manual).
